---
/**
 * How to report an error.
 *
 * No form, because the site has no backend and no client JavaScript, and a
 * contact form is the one thing that would need both. GitHub issues are public,
 * which is the point: a correction log that only the author can see is not a
 * correction log.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const REPO = "https://github.com/PhlegmaticPhox/ai-safety-index";
---

<Base
  title="Corrections | AI Safety Index"
  description="How to report an error, and what happens to it when you do."
  cites={[]}
>
  <section class="section section--open">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="masthead">
        <h1>Corrections</h1>
        <p class="masthead__meta mono">Public log, public repository</p>
      </div>

      <div class="doc">
        <p class="lede">
          Something here is wrong. On a site with this many numbers on it, that is a statement
          about statistics rather than about care. The useful question is what happens when
          somebody notices.
        </p>

        <h2>Report it</h2>
        <p>
          <a href={`${REPO}/issues/new`}>Open an issue</a> on the repository. It is public, so the
          report, the response and the fix are all visible without anyone having to take our word
          for it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Useful things to include: the page, the figure, and what you think it should say. If the
          error is in an upstream dataset rather than in what we did with it, say so, because the
          fix is different and usually involves telling them rather than us.
        </p>

        <h2>What happens then</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>An error in our processing</strong> is fixed in the pipeline and the change
            appears as a dated commit. The data files are committed to the repository, so what
            changed and when is visible in the diff.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>An error in an upstream source</strong> is not ours to fix. We will note it in
            that source's caveats on the <a href="/sources/">sources page</a>, which is where the
            provenance popover on every affected figure already points.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>A judgement you disagree with</strong>, such as how a jurisdiction is coded in
            the Governance Readiness Index, is worth raising even though it is not strictly an
            error. The coding rules and the instruments behind every score are published so that
            disagreement has something to bite on.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Rights holders</h2>
        <p>
          If you hold rights in something published here and want it removed, open an issue or
          contact us through the repository and we will remove it. This is deliberately easy: every
          source is declared in one registry and every record carries its identifier, so removing a
          source removes everything derived from it in a single change rather than a hunt.
        </p>

        <h2>Why it is logged publicly</h2>
        <p>
          A site whose entire argument is that you can check where a number came from cannot then
          patch its mistakes quietly. The repository history is the log: every data change, every
          correction and every judgement call is a dated commit with a message explaining it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
